  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "lesson-1",
      "name": "What is Machine Learning?",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What does it mean for a computer to learn?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore machine learning as an approach to AI in which a computer learns from data rather than being told what to do. Students consider how a model is trained on examples to make predictions, contrasting this approach with the logic-based AI they have seen in the previous unit. Students then train a machine learning model of their own to recognize the doodles they draw.",
        "student_overview": "**What does it mean for a computer to learn?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore machine learning as an approach to AI in which a computer learns from data rather than being told what to do. You'll consider how a model is trained on examples to make predictions and how this differs from the logic-based AI approach you saw in the previous unit. You'll then train a machine learning model of your own to recognize doodles."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-1",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-2",
      "name": "Lesson 2",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-2",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-3",
      "name": "Lesson 3",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-3",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-4",
      "name": "Prediction Models",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**Why are predictions useful?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore prediction as a way of navigating uncertainty, taking on the role of a small business owner running an ice cream shop. Students consider the kinds of questions a business owner might want answered and discover that machine learning offers two broad approaches to making predictions: regression, which predicts numerical data, and classification, which predicts categorical data. Students then experiment with a regression model and a classification model of their own, using them to make predictions about the shop.",
        "preparation": "- Open **Level 1: Regression** and **Level 3: Classification** to preview these widgets for students.",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**Why are predictions useful?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore prediction as a way of navigating uncertainty, taking on the role of a small business owner running an ice cream shop. You'll consider the kinds of questions a business owner might want answered and discover that machine learning offers two broad approaches to making predictions: regression, which predicts numerical data, and classification, which predicts categorical data. You'll then experiment with using a regression model and a classification model to make predictions about the shop."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-4",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-5",
      "name": "Lesson 5",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-5",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-6",
      "name": "Testing Regression Models",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do I know if one regression model is better than another?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn how to test and compare regression models. Students begin by informally judging two regression models against a set of data points, then confront the question of how to make that judgment precise. They then invent their own ways to quantify a model's \"loss\"—the errors it makes—and discover why some approaches can be misleading. Finally, they translate their thinking into Python by writing a loss function that compares two linear regression models on data of their choice, using the results to decide which model makes better predictions.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Exploring Loss Activity Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How do I know if one regression model is better than another?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn how to test and compare regression models. You'll begin by informally judging two regression models against a set of data points, then confront the question of how to make that judgment precise. You'll then invent your own ways to quantify a model's \"loss\"—the errors it makes—and discover why some approaches can be misleading. Finally, you'll translate your thinking into Python by writing a loss function that compares two linear regression models on data of your choice, using the results to decide which model makes better predictions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-6",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-7",
      "name": "Lesson 7",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-7",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-8",
      "name": "Building Classifiers",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can a computer make a prediction about something it’s never seen before?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore classification in more depth. Working as postmasters who want to know whether a day's mail will arrive late or on time, students discover the nearest neighbors algorithm: to label something new, look at the most similar examples you've already seen. They first apply the idea by hand on a single feature, then in code with multiple features. Students learn to represent a day's features as a Python dictionary, and explore how the prediction shifts as they change both the values of those features and the number of neighbors the classifier considers.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Nearest Neighbors Activity Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can a computer make a prediction about something it’s never seen before?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore classification in more depth. Working as postmasters who want to know whether a day's mail will arrive late or on time, students discover the nearest neighbors algorithm: to label something new, look at the most similar examples you've already seen. They first apply the idea by hand on a single feature, then in code with multiple features. Students learn to represent a day's features as a Python dictionary, and explore how the prediction shifts as they change both the values of those features and the number of neighbors the classifier considers."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-8",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-9",
      "name": "Testing Classifiers",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What makes one classifier better than another?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how to evaluate a classifier. Students consider three metrics—accuracy, recall, and precision—and compare two models that fail in different ways, discovering that the best classifier depends on the cost of each kind of mistake. Students then tune a classifier of their own, adjusting its confidence threshold to balance recall and precision in a context they choose.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Berry Picking Activity Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What makes one classifier better than another?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore how to evaluate a classifier. You'll consider three metrics—accuracy, recall, and precision—and compare two models that fail in different ways. You'll then tune a classifier of your own, adjusting its confidence threshold to balance recall and precision in a context of your choice."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-9",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-10",
      "name": "Sensing the World",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What does the world look like to a computer?**\n\nIn this lesson, students are introduced to how computers sense the world. Students first consider how computers \"see.\" They explore how a grid of bits can represent an image, coming to understand that computers sense the world through numeric representations. Students then consider how computers \"hear.\" They take on the problem of helping a computer tell two instruments apart, learning that the way a computer senses the world is determined by the way humans choose to represent it. As students build their own simple classifier, they see how those choices shape how well a computer can distinguish one instrument from another.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Seeing Through Bits Activity Guide (one for every two students). Optionally print copies of each variation, so different groups reveal different images.",
        "student_overview": "**What does the world look like to a computer?**\n\nIn this lesson, you're learn how computers sense the world. You'll first consider how computers \"see,\" then consider how computers \"hear.\" You'll then take on the problem of helping a computer tell two instruments apart, learning that the way a computer senses the world is determined by the way humans choose to represent it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-10",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
