      "ai_prompt": "",
      "seeding_key": {
        "understanding": 3,
        "learning_goal.key": "46058885-4102-4acc-a81f-ec9a8dea2936",
        "lesson.key": "lesson-1",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "understanding": 0,
      "teacher_description": "Student has not answered, or treats the model’s threshold as an arbitrary measure.",
      "ai_prompt": "",
      "seeding_key": {
        "understanding": 0,
        "learning_goal.key": "52ab8035-e43a-4a3a-94eb-85ba6cea0e4f",
        "lesson.key": "lesson-9",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "understanding": 1,
      "teacher_description": "Student explains where they set the model’s threshold, but offers only generic reasoning to support their response.",
      "ai_prompt": "",
      "seeding_key": {
        "understanding": 1,
        "learning_goal.key": "52ab8035-e43a-4a3a-94eb-85ba6cea0e4f",
        "lesson.key": "lesson-9",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "understanding": 2,
      "teacher_description": "Students explains where they set the model’s threshold, and offers a well-reasoned argument for why they chose such a threshold: for instance, by naming the metric they chose to prioritize.",
      "ai_prompt": "",
      "seeding_key": {
        "understanding": 2,
        "learning_goal.key": "52ab8035-e43a-4a3a-94eb-85ba6cea0e4f",
        "lesson.key": "lesson-9",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "understanding": 3,
      "teacher_description": "Students explains where they set the model’s threshold, and offers a well-reasoned argument for why they chose such a threshold: for instance, by naming the metric they were prioritizing. They also ground their reasoning in the particular context they chose, describing why the prioritization of that metric makes sense.",
      "ai_prompt": "",
      "seeding_key": {
        "understanding": 3,
        "learning_goal.key": "52ab8035-e43a-4a3a-94eb-85ba6cea0e4f",
        "lesson.key": "lesson-9",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "understanding": 0,
      "teacher_description": "Student has not answered, or treats the classifier’s prediction as a guess: they name a label without connecting it to past delivery dates, or give no reason about how many neighbors a nearest neighbors classifier should use.",
      "ai_prompt": "",
      "seeding_key": {
        "understanding": 0,
        "learning_goal.key": "7bb228a1-c3f7-4fa4-b9c9-384f2575a3fe",
        "lesson.key": "lesson-8",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "understanding": 1,
      "teacher_description": "Student explains that the classifier looks at the closest day (or days) and uses that label. They note that the number of neighbors changes the prediction, but justify the number only in general terms.",
      "ai_prompt": "",
      "seeding_key": {
        "understanding": 1,
        "learning_goal.key": "7bb228a1-c3f7-4fa4-b9c9-384f2575a3fe",
        "lesson.key": "lesson-8",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "understanding": 2,
      "teacher_description": "Student explains precisely, and in their own words, that the classifier predicts by finding the past days most similar to today, taking the most common label among them. They justify an optimal number of neighbors with reasoning grounded in what they saw.",
      "ai_prompt": "",
      "seeding_key": {
        "understanding": 2,
        "learning_goal.key": "7bb228a1-c3f7-4fa4-b9c9-384f2575a3fe",
        "lesson.key": "lesson-8",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "understanding": 3,
      "teacher_description": "Student explains precisely, and in their own words, that the classifier predicts by finding the past days most similar to today, taking the most common label among them. Student justifies an optimal number of neighbors with reasoning grounded in experimentation. They may also note that the optimal number of neighbors could depend on the data and the context in which the classifier is used.",
      "ai_prompt": "",
      "seeding_key": {
        "understanding": 3,
